---
import InsertLayout from '../../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import {
  KIT_ARCHIVE_PUBLISHED,
  KIT_DOWNLOAD_PATH,
  KIT_ON_SALE,
  KIT_RELEASE,
  KIT_TEST_COUNT,
  KIT_VERSION
} from '../../config/kit';
import '../../styles/kit.css';

const enc = KIT_RELEASE.encrypted_archive;
const zip = KIT_RELEASE.archive;
const root = `toledo-migration-acceptance-kit-${KIT_VERSION}`;
---

<InsertLayout
  title="Unpacking your Migration Acceptance Kit"
  description="How to decrypt, verify and run the Toledo Migration Acceptance Kit after purchase."
  noindex={true}
  heading="Unpacking your kit"
  subheading="Four commands. Nothing to install, no account, no login."
>
  <p class="kit-kicker">Release {KIT_VERSION}</p>

  {!KIT_ON_SALE && (
    <p class="kit-note">
      <strong>Nobody has bought this yet, because it is not on sale yet.</strong>
      This page is published early and on purpose, so anyone weighing the kit up
      can see exactly what delivery involves before deciding. Everything below
      is what the process will be; the download link appears here the moment the
      archive is actually published. See the
      <a href="/kit/">product page</a> for where the release currently stands.
    </p>
  )}

  <p>
    You should have a release key — Stripe showed it on the confirmation page
    immediately after payment. Keep it somewhere you will still have it in two
    years; it is the only thing standing between you and this archive, and
    there is no account to log in to and recover it from.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do not have it, skip to <a href="#lost">lost your key</a> at the
    bottom. It is a two-minute fix and it is not a problem.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Download the encrypted archive</h2>
  {KIT_ARCHIVE_PUBLISHED ? (
    <p>
      <a href={KIT_DOWNLOAD_PATH} download data-astro-reload><strong>{enc}</strong></a>
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p>
      It will be published at <code>{KIT_DOWNLOAD_PATH}</code>. It is not there
      yet, so there is no link here — a link to a file that does not exist is a
      404 pretending to be a delivery.
    </p>
  )}
  <p>
    This file is public and safe to be. It is AES-256 ciphertext, so the only
    thing that makes it useful is your key.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Decrypt it</h2>
  <p>
    macOS and Linux, in the folder where you downloaded it. <code>openssl</code>
    is already installed on both.
  </p>
  <div class="kit-command">openssl enc -d -aes-256-cbc -md sha512 -pbkdf2 -iter 600000 \
  -in {enc} \
  -out {zip}</div>
  <p>
    It prompts for the password rather than taking it as an argument, which is
    deliberate: your key does not end up in your shell history or in the process
    list. Paste the release key at the prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Windows:</strong> the same command, run in Git Bash (installed with
    Git for Windows, which includes <code>openssl</code>) or in WSL. Plain
    PowerShell does not ship <code>openssl</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Check you got what was advertised</h2>
  <div class="kit-command">shasum -a 256 {zip}          # macOS
sha256sum {zip}              # Linux
Get-FileHash {zip} -Algorithm SHA256   # PowerShell</div>
  <p>It should print, exactly:</p>
  <code class="kit-hash">{KIT_RELEASE.archive_sha256}</code>
  <p>
    That hash is also published on the
    <a href="/kit/">public product page</a>, where it was before you paid. If
    the two do not match, do not use the archive — email
    <a href="mailto:dev@toledotechnologies.com">dev@toledotechnologies.com</a>
    and say so, because it would mean something is wrong on our side.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Unzip and run the tests first</h2>
  <div class="kit-command">unzip {zip}
cd {root}
python3 -m unittest test_reconcile -v</div>
  <p>
    {KIT_TEST_COUNT} tests, about a tenth of a second, no network and no
    third-party packages. That they pass is the only warranty in the licence, so
    it is worth confirming before you rely on anything.
  </p>

  <h2>5. See it work</h2>
  <div class="kit-command">python3 reconcile.py \
  --spec specs/customers.json \
  --source examples/source-customers.csv \
  --target examples/target-customers.csv \
  --rejects examples/rejects-customers.csv \
  --out out</div>
  <p>
    Open <code>out/report.html</code>. The shipped example is built so that
    every bucket the tool can produce has at least one row in it — including the
    awkward ones: an orphan, a row declared excluded that migrated anyway, and
    duplicate keys on both sides. There is a test that fails if that stops being
    true.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then read <code>docs/ACCEPTANCE-PROCEDURE.md</code>. The tool is step 7 of
    twelve; the steps that decide whether a migration can be signed off at all
    happen before anyone migrates anything.
  </p>

  <h2>If decryption fails</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>bad decrypt</code> or <code>bad magic number</code> almost always
      means the key is wrong — usually a trailing space or a missing character
      from a copy-paste. Retype it rather than re-pasting.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check the whole download arrived: if the <code>.enc</code> file is much
      smaller than about {Math.round(KIT_RELEASE.archive_bytes / 1024)}&nbsp;KB,
      the download was truncated. Fetch it again.
    </li>
    <li>
      Very old <code>openssl</code> builds (1.0.x) do not support
      <code>-pbkdf2</code>. <code>openssl version</code> will tell you. Any
      current macOS, Linux or Git for Windows install is fine.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="lost">Lost your key</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:dev@toledotechnologies.com">dev@toledotechnologies.com</a>
    with your Stripe receipt reference and you will be sent the release key
    again. There is no charge and no limit on this; a key you cannot find is not
    a reason to lose access to something you bought.
  </p>

  <h2>Refunds, and what happens next</h2>
  <p>
    Within 30 days, email the same address with your receipt reference and ask
    for a refund. You get one. No reason required, no evidence, no questions.
    This is section 4 of <a href="/kit/license/">the licence</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Buyers of {KIT_VERSION} get 1.x maintenance releases at no additional cost.
    There is no mailing list and nothing to subscribe to — email with your
    receipt reference and you will be sent the current release.
  </p>
  <p>
    A reproducible defect gets fixed. Send the spec, the smallest input that
    reproduces it, and what you expected.
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
